+Evidence-guided review:
+Use the first execution for the complete scene, reserving a second execution for correction.
+Compare the rendered view against the original at two scales: broad composition and regional
+appearance first, then salient contours and parts. Check major boundaries, cropping, relative
+sizes, negative spaces, overlap order, silhouette profiles and regional brightness. Include
+background regions that occupy substantial pixels; plausible geometry alone does not match tone.
+Do not interpret render noise as missing geometric detail or copy photographic grain into geometry.
+
+Before revising, record briefly in program comments:
+- The largest directly observed discrepancies and regions already matching reasonably well.
+- Competing explanations when projection, geometry, placement or appearance remain ambiguous.
+- The parameter family chosen for correction, expected visible effect and regions at risk.
+- Checks actually performed and unresolved uncertainty.
+These comments describe the current reconstruction, not a claim of causal identification.
+
+Correlated landmark displacement across separated objects can motivate a camera check; a local
+silhouette discrepancy can motivate a component check. Neither establishes a unique cause.
+Separate camera pitch, focal length and translation from object scale, depth and component profile.
+If only one correction execution remains, choose a small coherent set of changes addressing the
+largest supported discrepancy. Avoid simultaneous speculative changes to camera, geometry and
+lighting, which obscure interpretation and can damage matching regions. Keep other parameter
+families stable unless independently observed errors require changes. If evidence is weak or
+conflicting, retain uncertainty and avoid a global correction justified by only one local mismatch.
+Do not spend the correction opportunity adding decorative detail while major composition errors remain.
+
+When controlled observations or metric decisions are supplied, use their actual recorded decision,
+including an unresolved outcome. Do not replace that decision with the initial hypothesis, the
+largest hypothesis weight or an intuitive visual diagnosis. A scalar measures the tested intervention
+under its stated conditions; it does not establish geometry truth or identify a fault module.
+A small negative effect does not rule out a parameter family, and a positive effect does not prove
+its causal correctness. Untested profile changes are not established by uniform-scale or depth tests.
+Keep score components, fixed evaluation regions and limitations distinct when available. Do not
+invent experiments, confidence thresholds or successful validation. An isolated-object view can
+clarify membership and silhouette; its full-image score does not justify deleting scene context.
+Approximate projected AABBs are not visible masks. Large bounds near or behind the camera can be
+projection artifacts; do not shrink structural geometry solely to fit such bounds.
+
+Preserve group structure and editability during every correction. Retain semantic assets, meaningful
+child parts, shared material families and declared support relationships. Do not flatten the scene,
+merge unrelated objects or hide difficult regions to improve a local comparison. Use the supplied
+scene program, if any, as evidence about construction choices, not as a replacement for the shared
+reconstruction procedure. Do not encode task identifiers, fixed image-specific coordinates or
+object-name-specific correction rules in the reconstruction procedure.
+
+Complexity and budget:
+The entire solve has three model calls, two Blender executions and 600 seconds. Runtime remaining
+budget governs whether a further call or execution is possible. Planning, corrections and review
+all consume that budget. Do not change the runner's resource policy or rendering settings to evade it.
